www.foobar.com
* -> explanations at the end.

When a user types www.foobar.com into their browser*, the first request path is the DNS server* of the website.
There, the domain* name www.foobar.com is associated with an A record* that maps a hostname into a IP* address (which is 8.8.8.8 in our case).
Once the browser obtains the IP address, it sends arequest to the server 8.8.8.8 using the TCP* over port* 443* or 80*.
Then, nginx*, which is the web server*, checks the requested domain and routes the request to application server*.
Why? Because nginx only serves static files, and dynamic content must be generated by the application server.
Inside the application server, the code written in PHP* queries the MySQL* database*.
After generating the HTML*, the application server sends it back nginx, and nginx sends the final response to the user's browser.
This is how most websites on the internet are accessed.


browser -> The programs to access web pages (like Chrome, Firefox, Safari).
DNS server -> Translates domain names into IP addresses so browsers know where to connect.
domain -> A human-friendly name for websites.
A record -> A DNS record that maps a hostname to an IPv4 address.
IP -> A unique numerical address that identifies a device on the internet.
TCP -> A reliable communication protocol used to send data between client and server.
Port -> A logical endpoint used for specific communications and services.
443 port -> default port for HTTPS.
80 -> default port HTTP (less secure version of HTTPS).
Nginx -> A web server that receives client requests and forwards dynamic requests to the application server.
PHP -> A server-side scripting language used to build dynamic web applications.
MYSQL -> A relational database that stores website data.
Database -> The system responsible for storing and retrieving structured information.
HTML -> HyperText Markup language used by browsers to display content.
